# ==============================================================================
# Local Café Menu SaaS — Environment Variables Template
# Dedicated Linux Server (Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS)
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ CRITICAL SECURITY WARNING:
# EVERY SINGLE placeholder value below MUST be replaced with your own unique,
# strong secrets before starting the application.
#
# The backend performs an automated security validation check at startup and
# will REFUSE TO START if any known placeholder or default values are detected.
# Never commit your active .env file or real secrets to version control.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Quick Setup:
# 1. Copy this template:
#      cp .env.example .env
# 2. Generate a secure 32-byte JWT secret:
#      openssl rand -hex 32
# 3. Fill in your PostgreSQL database credentials and generated JWT secret.
# 4. Set restrictive permissions:
#      chmod 600 .env
# ==============================================================================

# PostgreSQL Database Credentials (REQUIRED)
# Replace with a strong, random password. Do not leave the placeholder below!
POSTGRES_USER=cafe_admin
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace_with_your_strong_postgres_password_minimum_16_chars
POSTGRES_DB=cafe_menu

# JWT Secret for Cryptographically Signed Admin Sessions (REQUIRED)
# Generate using: openssl rand -hex 32
# Must be at least 16 characters and contain no placeholder words.
JWT_SECRET=replace_with_your_random_32_byte_hex_jwt_secret

# Host Entry Port (Optional, defaults to 80 for standard HTTP ingress)
HOST_PORT=80

# Stable Public HTTPS Base URL for Printed Table QR Codes (RECOMMENDED)
# e.g., https://menu.yourcafe.com
# Encoded directly into printed QR codes so customers on mobile 4G/5G reach your café.
PUBLIC_URL=https://menu.yourcafe.com

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supported Public HTTPS Method: Cloudflare Tunnel Secret Token
# REQUIRED ONLY IF USING: docker compose --profile tunnel up -d
#
# Steps:
# 1. In Cloudflare Zero Trust Dashboard -> Networks -> Tunnels, create a tunnel.
# 2. Add a Public Hostname pointing to Service: HTTP -> frontend:80.
# 3. Copy the tunnel token provided by Cloudflare and paste it below.
# 4. Launch with: docker compose --profile tunnel up -d
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=replace_with_your_actual_cloudflare_tunnel_token
